\section{Trace a Cut on an Eight-Bit Splitter}

Let $s$ be an eight-bit left rotation of $x$ by one bit. Take its two nibbles as $l=s\mathbin{\&}15$ and $h=s\gg4$, and return $l\mathbin{\mathtt{xor}}h$. The singleton rotation costs two units; extraction of either nibble and the final xor cost one each. The target also has two instructions costing two units each that rotate and extract just one nibble, and a different two-unit instruction that returns both $s$ and $l$. All costs are additive, all operations pure, and there are no other candidates. First assume a boundary value can travel in a virtual register without extra instructions.

\begin{verbatim}
x = 0xB3 -> s = 0x67 -> l = 7, h = 6 -> result = 1
cut at s:      ROT + LOW + HIGH + XOR        = 2+1+1+1 = 5
               # Both leaf uses read the same named s.
unfold at s:   ROTLOW + ROTHIGH + XOR        = 2+2+1   = 5
               # The two fused forms each rotate x.
retain DAG:    ROT_AND_LOW + HIGH + XOR      = 2+1+1   = 4
               # HIGH reads s exported by ROT_AND_LOW.
\end{verbatim}

The cover in \Cref{fig:visual-271} owns both the rotation and the low-nibble extraction, while still exporting the rotated byte for the high-nibble extraction.

