In a tree that unfolds $s$, the separate rotation and extraction would cost three at each consumer, so a tree selector chooses the two cost-two fused forms. A cut excludes \texttt{ROT\_AND\_LOW} because it would own both the producer and a node across the boundary. The full-DAG plan costs four and meets a lower bound: xor costs one, producing $l$ costs at least two including its rotation if necessary, and a distinct instruction must still produce $h$ for at least one more. If the cut interface instead requires one scratch store and one reload that both consumers can share, at unit cost each, the cut total rises to seven. That is a property of the chosen interface, not of edge splitting itself. The trace tests that an instruction claiming an exported $s$ really supplies it, and that neither the artificial leaf uses nor the duplicated occurrences are accidentally charged as independent materializations in the shared plan.
